<div class="center">
  <h1>Decision Curve Analysis</h1>
  <img src="dca_new_homepic_edited.png"
       alt="Decision curve analysis plot: net benefit vs threshold probability, comparing models to treat‑all and treat‑none">
</div>

<div class="content">
Decision curve analysis is a sophisticated statistical method that evaluates models and tests based on their clinical consequences. Unlike traditional accuracy measures such as the area‑under‑the‑curve or Brier score, decision curve analysis recognizes that not all errors are equal in medical diagnostics.
For instance, missing a cancer diagnosis (a false negative) can have far more serious implications than conducting an unnecessary biopsy (a false positive). This method provides a nuanced approach to model evaluation by calculating the net benefit of a model or test in comparison to two default strategies: treating all patients and treating no patients.

